**Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.**
Thanks for providing this neat calendar. I'm on v7.4.0 One issue: I would expect disabled days to be omitted from the default keyboard navigation order so that keyup and keydown events would focus the next / previous available day.

**Describe the solution you'd like**
A simple and general solution might be to provide a way to pass props into the Day component, perhaps by adding a key to modifier props. This would enable users to overwrite default properties on the Day component.

**Describe alternatives you've considered**
I tried to implement this myself using the onDayKeyDown handler:

```jsx
	onDayKeyDown = (day, m, e) => {
		const { fromDate, toDate } = this.state;
		const { key } = e
		if (key === 'ArrowDown' || key === 'ArrowRight' && day !== toDate) {
			this.focusNextDay(day);
			return;
		}
		if (key === 'ArrowUp' || key === 'ArrowDown' && day !== fromDate) {
			this.focusPreviousDay(day);
			return;
		}
	}
```
The issue I ran into is that the day that is being passed into this handler is not the day that was in focus during the key event - it's the day in focus after the key event navigates the user to the next / previous / up/ down day. 


**Additional context**
Thanks for considering.

In `react-day-picker` 10.0.1, changing the controlled `month` while a day has focus removes that day from the DOM and leaves `document.activeElement` on `<body>`. This breaks keyboard continuity for controlled calendars whose value can change externally, such as after undoing a date selection.

This also occurs on the current `main` branch. It was found while working on [WordPress/gutenberg#81635](https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/pull/81635).

## To reproduce

```tsx
import { useState } from "react";
import { DayPicker } from "react-day-picker";
import "react-day-picker/style.css";

const march = new Date(2026, 2, 15);
const november = new Date(2026, 10, 15);

export default function App() {
  const [month, setMonth] = useState(march);
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState<Date | undefined>(march);

  return (
    <div
      onKeyDown={(event) => {
        if (event.key.toLowerCase() === "j") {
          setMonth(november);
          setSelected(november);
        }
      }}
    >
      <p>Focus a day in the calendar, then press J.</p>
      <DayPicker
        mode="single"
        month={month}
        selected={selected}
        onMonthChange={setMonth}
        onSelect={setSelected}
      />
    </div>
  );
}
```

1. Use Tab to focus a day in March.
2. Press <kbd>J</kbd> to update the controlled month and selection to November.
3. Inspect the focus position or press an arrow key.

## Actual Behavior

November is displayed and November 15 is selected, but focus moves to `<body>`. Arrow keys no longer move between days.

The focus state in `useFocus` still refers to the day from the previous month. When that day is removed from the DOM, the browser has no replacement focus target.

## Expected Behavior

When a controlled month change removes the focused day, DayPicker should keep focus on an enabled day in the newly displayed month. The selected day is the natural target when it is enabled. DayPicker should not move focus when focus was outside the day grid before the update.

## Screenshots

Not applicable.

